p.src:
We begin with Kant as the philosopher whose emphasis on human dignity helped shape the moral language of human rights. Sandel’s first move is to show that this is not merely historical influence. In modern Germany, the first article of the constitution declares that human dignity is inviolable, never to be compromised. That is the lecture’s opening axiom: $\text{human dignity shall be inviolable}.$ At this stage, the claim stands as a principle, not yet as a solution. Sandel immediately gives it something to collide with: $\text{inviolable dignity}
\qquad \text{vs.} \qquad
\text{life-saving emergency}.$ That transition matters. The lecture does not begin by asking whether torture works. It begins by asking what becomes of an inviolable principle when the consequences of respecting it seem unbearable.

