If we write only the practical rationale of the threat, we get $\text{torture threat}
\Longrightarrow
\text{information about the child}.$ But the lecture’s point is that practical efficacy does not yet settle permissibility. Gaffgen receives a life sentence for murder, and yet the deputy police chief is also prosecuted and convicted for violating the kidnapper’s rights. So the case yields a sharper distinction: $\text{effective threat}
\neq
\text{permissible threat}.$ We should pause over the order here. Sandel wants the chronology to do philosophical work. First the urgency, then the threat, then the information, then the legal condemnation. That sequence is what makes the Kantian issue unavoidable.

