p.src:
The lecture now extracts the Kantian principle from the case. The argument against torture is not that the kidnapper is innocent. It is that there are certain inherent qualities in a person that are not forfeited even by terrible deeds. Torture uses a person to obtain something from him, even if the purpose is good. That is the point at which the lecture sharpens into means-and-ends language: $\begin{aligned}
\text{criminal cannot forfeit dignity}
&\Longrightarrow
\text{still protected against torture},\\
\text{use person as a means}
&\neq
\text{respect person as an end}.\end{aligned}$ The moral restriction is then stated in its strongest form: $\text{good purpose}
\not\Rightarrow
\text{permissible torture}.$ This is the lecture’s first major formal move. Sandel is not yet comparing totals of good and bad. He is clarifying the structure of the act itself. If the act treats a person merely as an instrument, then its purpose does not cleanse it.

