That pressure can be formalized as $\begin{aligned}
\text{if I can save the child and I do not}
&\Longrightarrow
\text{I am responsible for the child's death},\\
\text{respect for kidnapper's dignity}
&\stackrel{?}{=}
\text{failure to protect the child's dignity}.\end{aligned}$ The second line is not a settled equivalence. It is the question the utilitarian presses upon the Kantian. The lecture even puts the issue in terms of relative action: in some instances it will be good to torture, in others it will not. Sandel lets that challenge stand long enough for us to feel its force. A prohibition that ignores the child’s fate now looks, to the utilitarian, like a refusal to acknowledge responsibility for what one fails to prevent.

