p.src:
But Sandel is not finished. He raises the numbers. If one innocent girl could be tortured in order to save ten innocent children, the pressure changes: $\text{torture }1\text{ innocent}
\Longrightarrow
\text{save }10\text{ innocents}.$ As a shorthand for the aggregative pressure, we may write $10 > 1.$ This inequality is not the lecture’s proof. It is the temptation at the heart of the utilitarian response. Once the numbers rise, the speaker concedes that perhaps the right thing would be to torture one to save ten, because now it has become a matter of numbers in the end.

