The lecture advances by a sequence of increasingly sharp formal contrasts. It begins with the constitutional claim $\text{human dignity shall be inviolable},$ moves to the kidnapping case in which a torture threat seems justified by rescue, then states the Kantian restriction $\text{use person as a means}
\neq
\text{respect person as an end},$ and finally forces the utilitarian position into explicit aggregation: $\text{torture }1\text{ innocent}
\Longrightarrow
\text{save }10\text{ innocents}.$

